# Build output — regenerated by `npm run build`, never edited by hand.
dist/

# Astro's generated types and content cache.
.astro/

# Dependencies. `npm install` restores them from package-lock.json.
node_modules/

# Logs
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
*.log

# Environment
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Editor and OS clutter
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# ── DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED ──────────────────────────────────
# src/media/ is ~395MB of stills. That is source material, not build output:
# every work page and the mosaic bed are built from it, Astro optimises it at
# build time into dist/, and there is no other copy. It has to be in history.
#
# The largest single file is 23.8MB — Diwali's frame 03. Far inside GitHub's
# 100MB per-file limit, and the repository as a whole is around 480MB of
# history, which GitHub accepts. A first push and a fresh clone are both slow,
# and CI checks out shallow for exactly that reason.
#
# CLEARED 2026-10-06, when the project was prepared for upload: the six
# *-backup files at the root, the three dead .py generator scripts,
# src/pages/preview/ (72 drafts and their index), _originals/ (34MB: a
# broken-reflections folder that duplicated src/media byte for byte, and a
# unique roar folder), and src/media/about/portrait.PNG, which was 2.1MB that
# nothing read because PORTRAIT in src/data/about.ts is empty.
#
# CLEARED AGAIN 2026-10-12. A second library grew between 10-06 and 10-11 —
# the hero and bed drafts — and went the same way once d1 and c1 had been
# applied. Its last commit is ec65f33, which is the sha to use for any
# `/preview/ab-*` name written in a comment after 2026-10-06.
#
# THE FOLDER IS NOT IGNORED, DELIBERATELY. Ignoring it would let a draft be
# written and never noticed in `git status`; the build-time guard in
# astro.config.mjs is what keeps drafts off the live site, and it only works
# on a folder git can still see.
#
# All of it is in git history — the first library's last commit is 22fe932,
# the second's is ec65f33 — so nothing is lost, but note that REMOVING A FILE
# DOES NOT SHRINK .git. The
# blobs stay in history and a fresh clone still pulls them; only rewriting
# history would reclaim the space, and that changes every commit hash.
